#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tk-closure-check — verdict 5 of the merge gate, asked of the pull request.

Given a pull request and the queue item it belongs to, it says whether merging
that pull request will close THAT ticket, and exits non-zero naming WHICH
condition failed.

WHY THIS IS CODE AND NOT PROSE. `../skills/merge-gate/SKILL.md` states the
verdict, and a session reading it checked the body by eye. Every eye-check
measured so far asked PRESENCE where the rule asks IDENTITY: a body copy-pasted
from the previous slice carries a well-formed closing line for the wrong ticket
and passes; a Portuguese `Fecha #n` carries the right number under a word the
forge does not honour and passes; an owner half naming another account is
present, well-formed and wrong. All three merge green and close nothing, or
close somebody else's issue. The keyword has no undo.

FIVE CONDITIONS, each named in the output and each reported by itself:

  keyword   the word in front of the reference is one the forge honours. The set
            is `tk-ticket-ref.KEYWORDS`, and it is ENGLISH — that is the whole
            content of the check.
  number    a closing line in the body names the item's OWN ticket, repository
            half and number both.
  owner     the owner half is present, well-formed under the gate
            `bin/tracker-gh` applies, and is the tracker's own owner. Present
            and wrong is a failure, not a pass.
  extras    NOTHING ELSE in the body closes anything. The forge honours EVERY
            keyword-and-reference in a body, not the first one, so a second
            closing line closes a second ticket on merge — silently, with no
            undo, and the same two lines in the other order used to give the
            opposite verdict.
  base      the pull request targets its own repository's default branch. That
            is the condition under which the forge fires the keyword at all: a
            stacked pull request merged into its parent closes nothing, silently.

THE REFERENCE IS COMPOSED, NEVER RE-DERIVED. What the body is compared against is
the string `tk-ticket-ref` would PRINT for this item — owner and repository from
the tracker pair, number from the item — so this checker cannot greenlight a
reference the reader refuses to emit. Measured: an item naming `other-repo#9`
under the tracker `Owner/tracker-repo` is refused by the reader, because nothing
says who owns `other-repo`; a hand-written `Fixes Owner/other-repo#9` passed
every condition, since owner and repository were each checked against a different
source and the PAIR against none.

ROW NAMES, so that a caller greps for one rather than for prose: the five above,
plus `ticket` — the pre-condition, that the item's own reference could be
composed at all — and `item`, the line the escape quotes. Every row reads
`<name>  <state>  <detail>`, and a detail spanning lines is indented under its
own column.

THE ITEM DECIDES WHAT COUNTS, NEVER THE BODY ALONE. An item naming no ticket is
green only when the body closes nothing either — a body that closes something is
then closing a ticket this item never claimed. An item whose **Ticket:** no
reader may use is red with the remedy named, which is the exit verdict 5 owed
that state and did not have.

NO NETWORK IN THE OFFLINE MODE. `--pr` asks `gh` through PATH; `--body-file`
with `--base` and `--default-branch` answers the same five conditions from
values on hand, which is what makes the check runnable BEFORE the pull request
exists — the moment when rewriting the body costs nothing.

AN ITEM THAT LEFT THE QUEUE IS RED, NOT A CRASH. `AFK.md` step 5 closes a lane's
item after the pull request exists, and the merge gate asks verdict 5 later
still — so every item of an unattended package is already in the done-log when
this runs. The reader finds it there and says which log line to read, and the
row is FAILED: the item's **Ticket:** field left the queue with the item, and a
log line does not carry it, so the five conditions have no subject. An id the
queue never allocated is reported the same way. A verdict that could not be
answered is not a verdict that passed, and a gate that stops with no row leaves
the reader of an exit code alone with it.

EXIT CODES: 0 verdict 5 is green · 1 red, and the failing conditions are named ·
2 the run could not be made.
"""

import argparse
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

EXIT_GREEN = 0
EXIT_RED = 1
EXIT_FAILED = 2


def _load_reader():
    """`tk-ticket-ref` as a module, loaded by path like every suffix-less bin in
    this directory. It owns the item's ticket, the tracker's owner, the shape
    gates, the keyword set and the composition of the reference; this file asks
    it rather than keeping a second copy of any of the five."""
    path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "tk-ticket-ref")
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("tk_ticket_ref", path)
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("tk_ticket_ref", loader)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


reader = _load_reader()

# A word followed by a forge reference, anywhere in the body. The slug half is
# `\S*` rather than the slug alphabet on purpose: a MALFORMED owner half has to
# reach the checks to be reported as malformed, and a pattern that only matches
# well-formed slugs would read `Fixes owner|x/repo#1` as a bare `#1` and report
# the wrong defect.
#
# The colon is OPTIONAL and not absent: the forge's own documentation says a
# keyword may be followed by one, so `Fixes: #7` closes the ticket and a pattern
# demanding whitespace right after the word reads it as no closing line at all.
#
# The lookbehind excludes `-` as well as the word characters, because the forge
# honours a keyword and not a word ENDING in one: `Not-fixes owner/repo#7` closes
# nothing, and reading it as a closing line is a green verdict on a body that
# closes nothing.
MENTION_RE = re.compile(r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9_-])([A-Za-z]+):?[ \t]+(\S*)#([0-9]{1,9})(?![0-9])")

OK, FAILED = "ok", "FAILED"


def fail(msg):
    """Stop with code 2 — the run did not happen, which is not a red verdict."""
    print(f"tk-closure-check: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(EXIT_FAILED)


def gh(repo, *argv):
    """One `gh` call against the pull request's own repository. Resolved through
    PATH, never by an absolute path: that is what lets the suite put a fake `gh`
    in front of it and prove the run never reached the network."""
    try:
        run = subprocess.run(["gh", *argv], cwd=repo, capture_output=True, text=True)
    except OSError as e:
        fail(f"gh could not be run: {e}")
    if run.returncode != 0:
        fail(f"`gh {' '.join(argv)}` failed: "
             f"{(run.stderr or run.stdout).strip() or 'no output'}")
    try:
        return json.loads(run.stdout)
    except ValueError:
        fail(f"`gh {' '.join(argv)}` answered something that is not JSON: "
             f"{run.stdout.strip()[:200] or 'nothing'}")


def pull_request(args, clone):
    """(body, base, default branch) — from the forge, or from the flags.

    `--json` on the read is not a preference: `gh pr view <n>` without it walks
    the classic-Projects GraphQL path and dies on its deprecation notice, which
    `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md` records for this account's `gh`."""
    if args.pr is not None:
        pr = gh(clone.path, "pr", "view", str(args.pr), "--json", "body,baseRefName")
        repo = gh(clone.path, "repo", "view", "--json", "defaultBranchRef")
        default = (repo.get("defaultBranchRef") or {}).get("name") or ""
        if not default:
            fail("the forge named no default branch for this repository, so condition "
                 "`base` cannot be answered. It is not assumed to be `main`.")
        return pr.get("body") or "", pr.get("baseRefName") or "", default
    try:
        with open(args.body_file, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            body = f.read()
    except OSError as e:
        fail(f"{args.body_file} cannot be read: {e.strerror}")
    return body, args.base, args.default_branch


def mentions(body):
    """Every `<word> <slug>#<n>` in the body, as (word, slug, number)."""
    return [(m.group(1), m.group(2), m.group(3)) for m in MENTION_RE.finditer(body)]


def spell(mention):
    """One mention, as the body spells it — for a message that names a line."""
    word, slug, cited = mention
    return f"`{word} {slug}#{cited}`" if slug else f"`{word} #{cited}`"


def aims_at(slug, cited, repo, number):
    """Does a body's `<slug>#<cited>` aim at the expected `<repo>#<number>`?

    The repository halves are compared case-insensitively, because a forge repo
    name is case-insensitive to look up — two casings ARE one repository, which
    is what `tk-queue.canonical_ref` says of the same value.

    The NUMBER is compared as text, against the canonical spelling the reader
    emits. `#0007` is not a form the forge's own `#N` syntax resolves — the queue
    says so in as many words where it drops the leading zeros — so a body
    spelling it that way closes nothing, and reading it as this ticket is a green
    verdict on a line that fires at nothing.

    An ABSENT repository half aims here too: it is wrong for another reason, and
    the `owner` row is the one that says which."""
    half = slug.rpartition("/")[2]
    return cited == number and (not half or half.lower() == repo.lower())


def owner_verdict(slug, wanted):
    """(state, detail) for the owner half of a closing reference.

    Four states, and the fourth is the one a presence check cannot see: absent,
    malformed, PRESENT AND WRONG, right. An owner naming another account is a
    closing line the forge resolves — against that account's repository.

    `wanted` is the owner of the reference `tk-ticket-ref` composed, and the
    comparison is case-insensitive because a forge account name is
    case-insensitive to look up: a re-cased owner resolves to the same account,
    so refusing it would be a red verdict on a line that closes the ticket."""
    owner, sep, _ = slug.partition("/")
    if not sep:
        return "absent", (f"the reference is `{slug}#…`, with no owner half. Resolved "
                          "against the repository the pull request sits on, it closes an "
                          "unrelated issue of that repository, or nothing.")
    if not reader.TRACKER_RE.fullmatch(slug):
        return "malformed", (f"the owner-qualified half `{slug}` is not a GitHub slug — "
                             "letters, digits, dot, dash and underscore only, one slash.")
    if owner.lower() != wanted.lower():
        return "wrong", (f"the owner half is `{owner}`, and the ticket is owned by "
                         f"`{wanted}`. It is present and well-formed, which is exactly "
                         "why a presence check passes it: the forge resolves it, against "
                         "somebody else's repository.")
    return "right", owner


def conditions(body, base, default, expected, label):
    """[(name, state, detail)] — the five conditions, in a fixed order.

    Every one of them is reported, green or red, and that is deliberate: a gate
    that prints only its failures cannot be told from a gate that skipped a
    check. It holds in the keyword-red path too, which used to return one row and
    stop: `Fecha <the right reference>` is wrong in exactly ONE way, and a report
    that answered only the keyword left a session unable to tell that from a body
    whose reference is wrong as well.

    ALL the honoured mentions are read, never the first one. The forge fires on
    every keyword it finds, so the question is two questions — is the right line
    THERE, and is any OTHER closing line there — and reading `honoured[0]` asked
    a third one that has no answer: the same two lines in the other order gave
    the opposite verdict."""
    line = f"{reader.CLOSING_KEYWORD} {expected}"
    _, repo, number = split(expected)
    found = mentions(body)
    honoured = [m for m in found if m[0].lower() in reader.KEYWORDS]
    # the rows below read the HONOURED lines when there are any and the found
    # ones when there are none, so that a body under a word the forge does not
    # honour still gets its number and owner answered: one defect, one repair
    candidates = honoured or found
    aimed = [m for m in candidates if aims_at(m[1], m[2], repo, number)]
    right = [m for m in aimed if owner_verdict(m[1], split(expected)[0])[0] == "right"]
    subject = (right or aimed or candidates or [None])[0]
    strays = [m for m in honoured if not aims_at(m[1], m[2], repo, number)]

    out = []
    if honoured:
        out.append(("keyword", OK, subject[0]))
    elif subject is not None:
        out.append(("keyword", FAILED,
                    f"the body says {spell(subject)}, and `{subject[0]}` is not a keyword "
                    "the forge honours. The set is English and closed: "
                    f"{', '.join(reader.KEYWORDS)}. The line cites a ticket, reads like a "
                    "closing line, and closes nothing."))
    else:
        out.append(("keyword", FAILED,
                    f"no closing line in the body: nothing reads `<keyword> <ref>` for "
                    f"{label}'s ticket. The keywords are {', '.join(reader.KEYWORDS)}. "
                    f"The line to paste is `{line}`."))

    if aimed:
        out.append(("number", OK, expected))
    else:
        closes = f"; the body closes {', '.join(spell(m) for m in strays)}" if strays else ""
        out.append(("number", FAILED,
                    f"no closing line in the body names {label}'s ticket `{expected}`"
                    f"{closes}. A body copy-pasted from another slice closes that slice's "
                    f"ticket, and the merge looks exactly like a successful one. The line "
                    f"to paste is `{line}`."))

    if subject is None:
        out.append(("owner", FAILED,
                    "the body carries no reference at all, so there is no owner half to "
                    f"read. `{line}` is the whole line."))
    else:
        state, detail = owner_verdict(subject[1], split(expected)[0])
        out.append(("owner", OK, detail) if state == "right" else ("owner", FAILED, detail))

    if strays:
        out.append(("extras", FAILED,
                    f"the body also closes {', '.join(spell(m) for m in strays)}, which is "
                    f"not {label}'s ticket. The forge honours EVERY keyword and reference "
                    "in a body, not the first one, so merging this pull request closes "
                    "that ticket too — silently, and there is no undo."))
    else:
        out.append(("extras", OK, "no other closing line in the body"))

    if base != default:
        out.append(("base", FAILED,
                    f"the pull request targets `{base}`, and the repository's default "
                    f"branch is `{default}`. The forge fires the keyword only into the "
                    "default branch: merged anywhere else it closes nothing, silently."))
    else:
        out.append(("base", OK, base))
    return out


def split(qualified):
    """`<owner>/<repo>#<n>` taken apart — the three halves the rows compare."""
    owner, _, rest = qualified.partition("/")
    repo, _, number = rest.partition("#")
    return owner, repo, number


def report(rows, label):
    """The rows, one per line, and the verdict. A detail spanning lines is
    indented under its own column, so a row is one row to whoever reads it."""
    width = max(len(name) for name, _, _ in rows)
    for name, state, detail in rows:
        head = f"{name.ljust(width)}  {state}  "
        print(head + detail.replace("\n", "\n" + " " * len(head)))
    red = [name for name, state, _ in rows if state == FAILED]
    if red:
        print(f"verdict-5: RED for {label} — {', '.join(red)}")
        return EXIT_RED
    print(f"verdict-5: GREEN for {label}")
    return EXIT_GREEN


def refused(refusal, label):
    """A refusal from the reader, as the pre-condition row. The `[?]` item, the
    unconfigured clone, the path that is no clone and the ticket in another
    repository all reach verdict 5 the same way: red, with the defect and its
    remedy named. Verdict 5 had no exit for any of them, which left them
    permanently red and unactionable."""
    return report([("ticket", FAILED, f"{refusal.code}: {refusal.message}")], label)


def escape(block, body, label):
    """Verdict 5 for an item that names no ticket — the merge gate's own escape, and
    it is the ITEM that gives it. Every closing line is named, not the first: a
    body carrying two of them closes two tickets this item never claimed."""
    closing = [m for m in mentions(body) if m[0].lower() in reader.KEYWORDS]
    if closing:
        return report([("ticket", FAILED,
                        f"{label} names no ticket, and the body says "
                        f"{', '.join(spell(m) for m in closing)} — a closing line for a "
                        "ticket this item never claimed. Drop it, or point the item at "
                        "that ticket.")], label)
    # the row below is the quotation the digest is required to show
    return report([("ticket", OK, f"{label} names no ticket, and the body closes nothing"),
                   ("item", OK, block.splitlines()[0].strip())], label)


# What the reader's refusal cannot know: WHY verdict 5 wanted the item. The
# remedy is an ordering one, and it is the same for every closed item.
CLOSED_REMEDY = (
    "The five conditions have no subject: the item's **Ticket:** field left the queue "
    "with the item, and the log line does not carry it. Ask verdict 5 BEFORE the close — "
    "the pull request's body is written at `AFK.md` step 5 stage 6 and the item closes at "
    "stage 7 — or answer it by hand against the reference that body carries.")


def check(args):
    memdir = reader.queue_dir(args.dir)
    try:
        block, label = reader.item_block(memdir, args.id)
    except reader.Refusal as refusal:
        detail = f"{refusal.code}: {refusal.message}"
        if refusal.code == "closed":
            detail += "\n" + CLOSED_REMEDY
        return report([("ticket", FAILED, detail)], f"T{args.id:03d}")

    try:
        ticket = reader.item_ticket(block, label)
    except reader.Refusal as refusal:
        if refusal.code != "no-ticket":
            return refused(refusal, label)
        ticket = None

    # the clone is what `gh` is run in and what declares the owner, so it is
    # resolved before either is needed — and NOT resolved for an item with no
    # ticket read from a body on disk, where no clone is asked anything
    clone = None
    if ticket is not None or args.pr is not None:
        try:
            clone = reader.item_clone(block, label, args.repo)
        except reader.Refusal as refusal:
            return refused(refusal, label)

    if ticket is None:
        return escape(block, pull_request(args, clone)[0], label)

    try:
        expected = reader.qualify(ticket, reader.tracker_slug(clone, label), label)
    except reader.Refusal as refusal:
        return refused(refusal, label)

    body, base, default = pull_request(args, clone)
    return report(conditions(body, base, default, expected, label), label)


def main(argv=None):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="tk-closure-check",
        description="Verdict 5 of the merge gate: will merging this pull request close "
                    "THIS item's ticket? Checks the keyword, the ticket's identity, the "
                    "owner half, any OTHER closing line and the base branch, and names "
                    "the ones that failed.",
        epilog="Exit 0 green · 1 red, the failing conditions named · 2 the run could not "
               "be made.")
    parser.add_argument("id", type=reader.tk_queue.parse_id, metavar="ID",
                        help="the item, as T006, t006, 006 or 6")
    parser.add_argument("--dir", metavar="DIR",
                        help="the queue directory (default: this project's, by cwd)")
    parser.add_argument("--repo", metavar="DIR",
                        help="the clone the pull request lives on and whose `tk.tracker` "
                             "names the owner (default: the item's **Repo:** field, then "
                             "the current directory)")
    parser.add_argument("--pr", type=int, metavar="N",
                        help="the pull request number, read through `gh`")
    parser.add_argument("--body-file", metavar="PATH",
                        help="the body on disk, for a check made before the pull request "
                             "exists; needs --base and --default-branch")
    parser.add_argument("--base", metavar="BRANCH",
                        help="the branch the pull request targets, with --body-file")
    parser.add_argument("--default-branch", metavar="BRANCH",
                        help="the repository's default branch, with --body-file")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if (args.pr is None) == (args.body_file is None):
        parser.error("give exactly one of --pr and --body-file")
    if args.body_file is not None and not (args.base and args.default_branch):
        # a condition that quietly goes unasked is the defect this bin exists to
        # end, so the offline mode refuses rather than skipping `base`
        parser.error("--body-file needs --base and --default-branch: without them "
                     "condition `base` would go unasked, and an unasked condition "
                     "reads as a green one")
    return check(args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
